"""
CTSA Epilepsy — Galactosialidosis / Combined Neuraminidase-β-Galactosidase Deficiency / Protective Protein Cathepsin A Deficiency
=====================================================================================================================================
40-patient cohort · CTSA (20q13.12) · Autosomal recessive (AR) biallelic LOF
CTSA encodes Cathepsin A / Protective Protein (PPCA); 480 aa precursor ~54 kDa (signal peptide 1-26;
propeptide 27-97; mature two-chain: heavy ~32 kDa + light ~20 kDa held by disulfide); lysosomal
serine carboxypeptidase (S10 family; Ser150-His429-Asp372 catalytic triad); DUAL FUNCTION:
  (1) Serine carboxypeptidase: cleaves C-terminal amino acids from peptides
  (2) PROTECTIVE PROTEIN (non-catalytic) for NEU1 and GLB1:
       — Required for NEU1 (α-neuraminidase 1) activation, folding, and lysosomal stability
       — Required for GLB1 (β-galactosidase) protection from premature intralysosomal degradation
CTSA LOF → BOTH NEU1 AND GLB1 secondarily deficient → COMBINED enzyme deficiency
→ sialyloligosaccharide + GM1 ganglioside + keratan sulfate accumulation → galactosialidosis.

GALACTOSIALIDOSIS — THREE PHENOTYPIC FORMS:
════════════════════════════════════════════
(1) EARLY INFANTILE (severe): Fetal hydrops / neonatal ascites / hepatosplenomegaly;
    coarse facies; profound neurological impairment; cherry-red spot; survival ≤1y.
    Biallelic severe (null) CTSA variants; Japanese founder: p.Tyr220Cys has late-infantile NOT this form.
(2) LATE INFANTILE (intermediate): Moderate developmental delay; hepatosplenomegaly;
    coarse facies; cherry-red spot; myoclonus onset 1-3y; survival to childhood/adolescence.
(3) JUVENILE/ADULT (attenuated — THIS DASHBOARD FOCUS): PME phenotype; onset 10-30y (mean 15y);
    cherry-red macular spot (90-95%); action myoclonus (90%); GTCS (70%); cerebellar ataxia (75%);
    COARSE FACIES (80%); ANGIOKERATOMA (65%; trunk/pelvis); MILD INTELLECTUAL DISABILITY (60%);
    hepatosplenomegaly (50%); short stature (55%); vertebral deformities (40%).
    KEY: cognition MILDLY IMPAIRED (not NORMAL as in Sialidosis Type I).
    Japanese founder variants: p.Ser23Leu + p.Gly411Ser.
    Survival to 5th-6th decade with care; longer than early/late infantile forms.
    OMIM: *116130 (CTSA gene) / #256540 (galactosialidosis)

CTSA PROTEIN BIOLOGY:
═════════════════════
CTSA (Cathepsin A / PPCA / Lysosomal Carboxypeptidase A; 20q13.12):
  - 480 aa precursor (~54 kDa); signal peptide aa 1-26 (ER targeting); propeptide 27-97 (autocatalytic removal)
  - Mature form: heavy chain (aa 98-299; ~32 kDa) + light chain (aa 316-480; ~20 kDa)
    held by single disulfide bond (Cys241-Cys342); active-site pocket between two chains
  - Serine carboxypeptidase (S10 family; Cbh homologue superfamily)
    Catalytic triad: Ser150 (nucleophile) - His429 - Asp372
  - Two distinct functions in lysosomes:
    (a) Carboxypeptidase: removes C-terminal amino acids from substrates (including deamidation)
    (b) PROTECTIVE PROTEIN: stabilises NEU1 and GLB1 within the 1.3 MDa multienzyme complex
        NEU1: requires CTSA for proper folding (activates apo-NEU1 → active NEU1)
        GLB1: requires CTSA to prevent premature intralysosomal cathepsin-mediated degradation
  - CTSA LOF → apo-NEU1 remains inactive + GLB1 rapidly degraded → BOTH NEU1+GLB1 deficient
  - Combined NEU1+GLB1 deficiency = PATHOGNOMONIC biochemical fingerprint of galactosialidosis
  - pLI ~0.89 (high LOF intolerance)
  - Discovery: d'Azzo A et al. 1982 Proc Natl Acad Sci — first identification of 'protective protein'
    deficiency in galactosialidosis fibroblasts (before gene cloning); Galjart NJ et al. 1988 Cell —
    cloning of human CTSA/PPCA gene; Bonten E et al. 1996 — NEU1-CTSA-GLB1 complex characterisation

COMBINED NEURAMINIDASE + β-GALACTOSIDASE DEFICIENCY — DIAGNOSTIC CORNERSTONE:
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
CTSA LOF → simultaneous deficiency of TWO lysosomal enzymes:
  1. α-Neuraminidase 1 (NEU1) — cleaves terminal sialic acid from glycoconjugates
  2. β-Galactosidase (GLB1) — cleaves terminal galactose from glycoconjugates
ACCUMULATES:
  (a) Sialyloligosaccharides (from NEU1 deficiency — same as sialidosis)
  (b) GM1 ganglioside and its asialylated derivatives (from GLB1 deficiency)
  (c) Keratan sulphate fragments (from GLB1 deficiency)
DIAGNOSTIC RULE:
  NEU1 only deficient → Sialidosis (NEU1 mutation)
  GLB1 only deficient → GM1 Gangliosidosis / MPS IVB (GLB1 mutation)
  BOTH NEU1 + GLB1 deficient → GALACTOSIALIDOSIS (CTSA mutation) — the only condition causing both
WES panel: MUST include CTSA + NEU1 + GLB1 simultaneously to avoid diagnostic error.

CLINICAL COMPARISON WITH SIALIDOSIS TYPE I (KEY DIFFERENTIAL — MOST COMMON CONFUSION):
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
                         Sialidosis Type I          Galactosialidosis (Juv/Adult)
Gene:                    NEU1 (6p21.33)             CTSA (20q13.12)
Enzyme deficient:        NEU1 only                  BOTH NEU1 + GLB1
Cognition:               NORMAL (defining feature)  MILDLY IMPAIRED (60%)
Coarse facies:           ABSENT                     PRESENT (80%)
Angiokeratoma:           ABSENT                     PRESENT (65%; trunk)
Cherry-red spot:         YES (90-95%)               YES (90-95%)
Action myoclonus:        YES (98%)                  YES (90%)
Hepatosplenomegaly:      Absent (mild at most)      Present (50%)
Short stature:           No                         Yes (55%)
DX test order:           Urine sialyloligosaccharides first → Leukocyte NEU1 ONLY low
                                                    Urine sialyloligosaccharides → BOTH NEU1+GLB1 low
VPA safety:              SAFE (lysosomal NEU1)      SAFE (lysosomal CTSA/protective protein)
VGB:                     HIGH RISK (cherry-red macular)  HIGH RISK (cherry-red macular)
Key founder:             Italian / North African    Japanese (p.Ser23Leu + p.Gly411Ser)
"""


